---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import DocTerminal from '../../components/DocTerminal.astro';
import Continue from '../../components/Continue.astro';
import batteryTrace from '../../data/trace-battery-loop.json';
import carVerdicts from '../../data/trace-car-two-verdicts.json';
import laptopLenses from '../../data/trace-laptop-two-lenses.json';
import { implementationNav } from '../../lib/sections';

const runShell = `git clone https://github.com/unidpp/unidpp-core
cd unidpp-core

cargo run -p unidpp-demo -- --list
cargo run -p unidpp-demo -- --scenario battery-loop
cargo run -p unidpp-demo -- --scenario car
cargo run -p unidpp-demo -- --scenario laptop

# every scenario twice, byte-identical output asserted
cargo test --workspace`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Demonstrations"
  description="The UniDPP reference core, running: the narrated battery-loop demonstration with its verbatim CLI trace, the same record graded twice (car as-of readings, laptop two-lens verdicts), and how to run every scenario yourself."
  eyebrow="Evidence — demonstrations"
  sectioned
>
  <Sidebar slot="rail" heading="Implementation" items={implementationNav} />
  <h1>Demonstrations</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The reference core ships with a demonstration binary. Each scenario replays a product
    life through commitments, transformations, and verdicts — and prints what a verifier
    sees at every step. The traces below are <strong>generated, not transcribed</strong>:
    every timestamp is fixed, every salt is derived from the run seed, and the test suite
    runs every scenario twice and asserts byte-identical output.
  </p>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="battery-h">
    <h2 id="battery-h">The battery loop</h2>
    <p>
      Cells become a pack; the pack is harvested, installed, stolen from, recycled, packed
      onto a carrier, and graded — the transformation algebra, the blind-edge discipline,
      graph taint, end-of-waste, and the degradation ladder in one loop. The narration
      below is keyed to the step markers in the trace that follows it.
    </p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">1–2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Issuance of the cells</h3>
          <p>
            Two cell passports open at lot/serial granularity — the finest identifier
            granularity available, with dormant identifiers allowed — each sealed by an
            unsalted commitment to the canonical body. Any later in-place edit is
            detectable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Combine, N&nbsp;&#8594;&nbsp;1</h3>
          <p>
            The pack-maker issues <code>pack-1</code> as a derived passport whose issuance
            carries input references pinned by as-of state hashes — a Git-merge topology.
            Mass balance runs in the ISO&nbsp;80000 unit registry: 25&nbsp;kg in, 24.2&nbsp;kg
            out, 0.8&nbsp;kg loss; <code>in&nbsp;&#8722;&nbsp;out&nbsp;=&nbsp;loss</code> is auditable, and the
            output quantity is a new measured fact, never a copy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">4</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Split, 1&nbsp;&#8594;&nbsp;N: harvest</h3>
          <p>
            The harvester carves a 10&nbsp;kg module out of the pack; the parent retains the
            remainder and is not consumed. Sum of children bounded by the parent, enforced
            by remainder semantics.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">5</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Blind installation into the car</h3>
          <p>
            The child's log records the installation interval — but the parent identity
            appears only as <code>H(salt&nbsp;&#8214;&nbsp;car-id)</code>, and the salt is consumed by the
            commitment and dropped. The serialized log provably contains neither the car
            identity nor the salt (checked at runtime); a disclosure ceremony recomputes
            the binding to <code>true</code> for the true parent and <code>false</code> for any other.
            Proof-of-binding is not knowledge-of-parent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">6</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Theft on a cell: taint through the graph</h3>
          <p>
            A fraud-class security flag on one cell is <strong>retroactive</strong>: it
            voids validity ab initio over an explicit window and propagates through the
            provenance DAG. The pack and the harvested module both void — stolen material
            cannot be laundered through transformation — while the clean sibling branch is
            unaffected. The recall set is derived by trace-down navigation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">7</span>
        <div>
          <h3>End-of-waste</h3>
          <p>
            An accredited recycler appends an end-of-waste event with an evidence
            reference; replaying the log moves the status through a legal state-machine
            transition. The passport is never rewritten, only appended to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">8</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Tier-A packing against the QR budget</h3>
          <p>
            The log is projected onto the carrier-embedded offline minimum — product
            identifier, resolver URI, status, safety flag, log head, two
            multi-suite signature slots (ECDSA&nbsp;P-256 + SM2) — and budgeted
            against the ISO/IEC 18004 byte-capacity tables. This scenario frames
            the slots as placeholders at canonical suite lengths, so the verdict
            degrades with the stated reason <em>signatures-framed-only</em>;
            computed co-signatures over one body — the sovereign-suite packs the
            issuer mints under <code>suite=ecdsa-p256,sm2</code> — fill both
            slots and verify under each suite:
          </p>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table class="register">
              <caption>
                Substituting ML-DSA-65 (a 3309-byte post-quantum signature) for the two
                classical suites projects to 3570&nbsp;B against a 2331&nbsp;B ceiling at QR
                v40-M — rejected with an over-budget error, never silently truncated.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>encoded</td><td>277&nbsp;B</td></tr>
                <tr><td>projected (signature placeholders at canonical suite lengths)</td><td>405&nbsp;B</td></tr>
                <tr><td>carrier</td><td>QR version 15, EC level M, capacity 412&nbsp;B</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>margin</strong></td><td><strong>+7&nbsp;B</strong></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">9</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Profile applicability by predicate</h3>
          <p>
            The EU battery profile triggers on a predicate over twin facts —
            <code>battery.capacity-kwh&nbsp;&gt;=&nbsp;2</code> — and the twin's measured 2.5&nbsp;kWh
            satisfies it. The lens attaches by predicate, not by enumeration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">10</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The graded verdicts</h3>
          <p>
            Verification is graded, never boolean: current-state is
            <span class="neg">DEGRADED (signatures-framed-only)</span> with the taint
            reported as <span class="neg">VOIDS AB INITIO</span>; the evidentiary reading
            goes stale on the ladder and says so; offline verification without a cached
            anchor degrades rather than fails; and a single edited character in the
            serialized log is <span class="neg">FAIL (broken-chain)</span> — integrity
            failures are failures, not degradations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <DocTerminal
      label="unidpp-demo --scenario battery-loop"
      meta="seed 0x0000756e69647070 · lib unidpp-core 0.1.0 · exit 0"
      ariaLabel="Verbatim output of the battery-loop demonstration run"
      text={batteryTrace.text}
      caption="
        Verbatim output of the demonstration binary in the unidpp-core workspace,
        generated by cargo run -p unidpp-demo -- --scenario battery-loop. The seed
        drives deterministic salt derivation only; production salts must come from a
        CSPRNG. Identical seeds produce byte-identical traces, asserted by the
        workspace test suite.
      "
    />
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="verdicts-h">
    <h2 id="verdicts-h">One record, two verdicts</h2>
    <p>
      Law needs more than one reading of the same record, and lenses disagree. Two
      excerpts: the car's battery log graded at two verification times, and the laptop's
      single passport graded under two jurisdiction lenses at the same time. In both,
      both verdicts stand side by side — neither overrides the other, and the reason is
      stated, not implied.
    </p>

    <DocTerminal
      label="unidpp-demo --scenario car"
      meta="step 7 of 8 · excerpt"
      ariaLabel="Car scenario excerpt: as-of readings then and now"
      text={carVerdicts.text}
      caption="
        The evidentiary reading protects a good-faith actor who verified before the
        recall campaign; the current-state reading carries the recall forward, with
        framed-only signatures degrading explicitly.
      "
    />

    <DocTerminal
      label="unidpp-demo --scenario laptop"
      meta="step 8 of 9 · excerpt"
      ariaLabel="Laptop scenario excerpt: two lenses, two verdicts"
      text={laptopLenses.text}
      caption="
        The same passport passes the EU lens and degrades under the JP lens, naming the
        missing data point. Inter-profile disagreement is resolved to canonical facts
        plus each lens's rules — never merged into one true classification.
      "
    />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="run-h">
    <h2 id="run-h">Run it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      The demonstration binary is part of the <code>unidpp-core</code> workspace
      (<a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a>, Apache-2.0 / MIT).
      <code>--list</code> enumerates scenarios; <code>--seed&nbsp;0x&lt;hex&gt;</code> varies the
      salt derivation; the exit code is 0 on a completed run and 2 on a usage error. The
      test suite executes every scenario twice and asserts byte-identical output, checks
      that a changed seed moves the salted commitments while the car identity still never
      leaks, and exercises the usage-error codes — so a printed trace is also a passing
      conformance run.
    </p>
    <DocTerminal
      label="shell"
      meta="from a fresh clone"
      ariaLabel="Commands to clone unidpp-core and run the demonstrations"
      text={runShell}
    />
  </section>

  <Continue
    href="/implementation/conformance/"
    title="Conformance evidence"
    desc="Every example payload printed in EN 18223:2026, run through the validators — findings recorded as a factual register."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--prose);
  }
  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3.4rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 0.95rem 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .step-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 0.15rem;
  }
  .steps h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  }
  .steps p {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }
  .steps .tablewrap {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.55rem;
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
</style>
